hi welcome to omixlogic studies in  biology medicine and ecology are  becoming increasingly more complex  many of these studies rely on huge data  sets and advanced computational methods  like machine learning define interesting  relationships between dna mutations and  cancer or protein structures that inform  vaccine design to prevent viral  infections  it's fascinating to hear about these  studies but did you know that many of  the authors share their data by making  it publicly available the majority of  them are actually required to do so  so instead of going to the lab to try  and reproduce these studies ourselves we  can just take the data analyze it and  make our own discoveries think about  this  after collecting thousands of molecular  profiles from tumors labs deposit  genomic data into a single location for  anyone to analyze you and i can find  these data sets generated in  professional labs using expensive  equipment and simply download them  now if only we had the skills and  computational resources needed to  analyze these huge data sets  well  you can  that's what this portal is really about  scientific inquiry using complex  biological data collectively we call it  omics genomics transcriptomics  metagenomics epigenomics data that  describes in detail how biological  systems operate at the molecular level  in these lessons you'll find practical  tutorials quizzes exercises and coding  challenges i hope i didn't just lose you  there yes coding it can be intimidating  at first but omits logic makes it simple  the portal includes an interactive  console with code you can run directly  in your browser and a big data analysis  platform you can use to process  terabytes of data on the cloud this is  where you can learn to analyze data  using complex algorithms and even start  coding on your own in r or python  the portal is designed for people just  like you  curious minds that love to ask questions  find answers and showcase your findings  to the world  sign up is free and getting started is  quick  if you're new to this field i recommend  getting started by signing up completing  your profile and starting your journey  with two free courses  the first one is called introduction to  bioinformatics and the second one is  bytes and molecules this is where you'll  brush up on the basics to understand how  data is generated or used in various  applications  it's free and completing the course will  earn you a certificate that you can add  to your resume it'll also prepare you  for more advanced coursework case  studies sourced from impactful research  articles and mentor-guided training  programs on each of the topics you learn  about so why wait let's get started
 

